 "<0>In-context (working)</0>": "",
 "<0>Lives in:</0> Inside the model call: the token sequence the model attends to right now.": "",
 "<0>Lifetime / cost:</0> Lost when the call ends. Bounded by the context window. Costs tokens (and latency) every turn.": "",
 "<0>Example:</0> <1>The system prompt, tool schemas, the last N messages, tool results.</1>": "",
 "Rule of thumb: <0>only the context window is \"seen\" by the model</0>. Everything else must be <1>retrieved</1> and pasted into it. Cost grows with what you paste back, see <2>cost & caching</2>.": "",
 "▼ Dive in: <0>Memory as tools</0>": "",
 "<0>Short-term (conversation)</0>": "",
 "<0>Lives in:</0> In your application: a list of messages (database row, in-process list, session store).": "",
 "<0>Lifetime / cost:</0> Lives as long as the session. Re-sent to the stateless API each turn; compacted when long.": "",
 "<0>Example:</0> <1>The chat transcript for this thread.</1>": "",
 "<0>Long-term: semantic</0>": "",
 "<0>Lives in:</0> External store: key-value, document store or vector index.": "",
 "<0>Lifetime / cost:</0> Persists across sessions. Retrieved by key or similarity search; written explicitly.": "",
 "<0>Example:</0> <1>\"User is vegetarian\", \"Prod DB is Postgres {#}\".</1>": "",
 "<0>Long-term: episodic</0>": "",
 "<0>Lives in:</0> External store of past episodes: logs, trajectories, summaries of earlier sessions.": "",
 "<0>Lifetime / cost:</0> Persists; usually retrieved by recency or similarity to the current situation.": "",
 "<0>Example:</0> <1>\"Last time deploy failed because the migration was not run.\"</1>": "",
 "<0>Long-term: procedural</0>": "",
 "<0>Lives in:</0> Instruction files, skills, rules, learned prompts. Often plain files in the repo.": "",
 "<0>Lifetime / cost:</0> Persists; loaded on demand. Changing it changes the agent's behaviour without retraining.": "",
 "<0>Example:</0> <1>A CLAUDE.md-style file, a SKILL.md, a runbook.</1>": "",
 "A common pattern: expose memory as <0>tools</0>, so the model decides when to read and write. The harness executes them like any other tool call (<1>round trip</1>).": "",
